Experimental source-derived BORE CFS adaptation
==============================================

Provenance and scope
--------------------
Base tree: e79065c41a09ffa38baa6547f13c1471ce003695.
Algorithm source: Baka 0b1b6bdf6ce9ca6bba51cb9c9b918e4f027474f3,
kernel/sched/bore.c and scheduler integration from that commit.
This is an experimental source-derived CFS adaptation, NOT the complete
upstream BORE 7 scheduler or an EEVDF backport. No ADIOS, Cambyses or POC
algorithm is imported by the BORE adaptation. Optional boot defaults for BORE
and POC are described below; their algorithms remain unchanged. No defconfig
is changed.

Enable and concurrency contract
-------------------------------
CONFIG_SCHED_BORE defaults to n. CONFIG_SCHED_BORE_DEFAULT_ON depends on
CONFIG_SCHED_BORE and defaults to n. The boot-fixed enable flag is initialized
from CONFIG_SCHED_BORE_DEFAULT_ON (off when n, on when y). Pass sched_bore=0
or sched_bore=1 on the kernel command line to override either default for that
boot. Invalid values leave the existing default intact. Numeric 0/1 are accepted
(base-0 parsing). The sysctl kernel.sched_bore is read-only (0444), backed by
__ro_after_init storage. There is no supported hot enable/disable, no task
walk and no generation reset. Production cannot re-enable stale state.
A future live disable would stop only future scaling: historical vruntime
would NOT be rolled back. Do not make the sysctl writable without designing
that transition and state reset first.

Each accounting call takes one enable snapshot for both burst update and
scaling; sleep and fork also take one snapshot. The enable flag is immutable
after boot. Other tunables use READ_ONCE once per helper calculation, but
multiple independent sysctl writes are NOT an atomic tuning transaction.
State updates run under the existing rq scheduling serialization; fork
initializes a private child and reads the running parent's penalty. No
additional cross-task scanning, locks or policy-class changes are added.

Tunables (under /proc/sys/kernel)
--------------------------------
sched_burst_penalty_offset: 0..255, default 24.
sched_burst_penalty_scale: 0..65535, default 1536.
sched_burst_smoothness: 0..16, default 1 (exact supported shift range).
sched_burst_inherit_type: 0..1, default 1. 0 means no inheritance; 1 copies
only the direct parent's effective penalty. No ancestor/tree/cache modes
are implemented or advertised. Fork always clears burst/current state,
including when disabled. All bounds exist with CONFIG_SCHED_DEBUG=n.

Fixed-point math (source-compatible, not an exact logarithm)
----------------------------------------------------------
For burst time n > 0, let L = fls64(n) in [1,64]. The source interpolation is
  q = (L << 8) | (((n << (64-L)) mod 2^64) >> 56)
  current = min(10239, floor(max(0,q - offset*256) * scale / 1024)).
q and penalties use Q8 units: 256 = 1. The normalized mantissa byte includes
the leading 1 bit (128..255); q is NOT floor(256*log2(n+1)). We intentionally
retain this source convention rather than silently substituting another
logarithm. n=0 returns 0 before computing shifts; 64-L is always 0..63.
Burst accumulation saturates at U64_MAX instead of wrapping.

For smoothing shift s in [0,16],
  smooth(previous,current) = floor((previous*(2^s-1)+current)/2^s).
Sleep saves this smoothed penalty, clears current and burst, and sets the
effective penalty to the saved value. During execution effective is
max(current, smooth(previous,current)); previous is not advanced on ticks.
At s=0 history is discarded. At s=16 all intermediates still fit in u32
for penalties <=10239 (even the entire u16 input range is safe).

Integer scaling deliberately drops the Q8 fractional byte:
  k = min(effective >> 8,39)
  virtual_delta = sat_u64(floor(CFS_delta * (40+k) / 40)).
Thus the multiplier is 1..1.975, not an upstream BORE nice-weight mapping.
Write CFS_delta = 40*a+b, b<40. Compute tail=floor(b*(40+k)/40).
If a > floor((U64_MAX-tail)/(40+k)), return U64_MAX; otherwise return
 a*(40+k)+tail. This avoids overflow BEFORE multiplication. Capping an
already-overflowed product (as in the source) does not work.

Default offset=24, scale=1536 golden burst -> current penalty:
  0 -> 0
  1 -> 0
  8388607 (2^23-1) -> 0
  8388608 (2^23) -> 192
  16777216 (2^24) -> 576
  33554432 (2^25) -> 960
  U64_MAX -> 10239
Starting from zero, runtime=33554432 and CFS_delta=1000 produce 1075;
sleep then leaves previous/effective=480 and current/burst=0 at s=1.

Hooks and preserved paths
-------------------------
core.c __sched_fork: explicitly clears all BORE state for both init_idle and
sched_fork, even with BORE boot-disabled. Thus idle initialization never keeps
copied penalties or inherits a parent's state. This shared reset runs before
sched_fork can reject a deadline-policy child. core.c sched_fork subsequently
calls bore_fork after init_entity_runnable_average, before class task_fork;
only this normal fork path inherits the direct parent's effective penalty when
boot-enabled and inherit_type=1. The penalty algorithm is unchanged.
fair.c update_curr: only task entities route their already nice-weighted
calc_delta_fair result through bore_account_delta. Group entities retain the
original calc_delta_fair path. sum_exec_runtime, exec_clock, cpuacct, group
runtime and CFS bandwidth continue to receive unscaled real delta_exec.
No WALT, Uclamp, load tracking, wakeup placement or calc_delta_fair global
semantics are changed. This does NOT prove workload-level equivalence when
BORE is enabled: task shares and scheduling order intentionally change.
fair.c dequeue_entity: immediately after update_curr, gated by DEQUEUE_SLEEP
and entity_is_task. It does not depend on schedstats. Migration/requeue
without DEQUEUE_SLEEP does not terminate a burst.

With boot-off, bore_account_delta returns exactly the original CFS delta
without touching burst state, and sleep is a no-op. Compiled-in off still
has call/branch overhead and task_struct storage; it is not binary-identical
to CONFIG_SCHED_BORE=n. Existing vruntime unsigned addition/wrap semantics
are unchanged. Saturation protects the delta multiplication, not arbitrary
unbounded synthetic vruntime additions; real update_curr rejects nonpositive
signed deltas. No stronger global wrap guarantee is claimed.

Persistent host tests (no kernel build)
--------------------------------------
  python3 -I tools/testing/selftests/bore/run.py
or
  make -C tools/testing/selftests/bore run_tests

The runner extracts actual bore.c C and the actual bore_ctx definition,
provides only kernel API/type shims, and compiles the real algorithm, fork,
sleep and boot parser with GCC. It does not reimplement BORE in Python.
Both ordinary and ASan+UBSan binaries run normal/edge/golden/fork/off tests,
200000 deterministic u128 multiplication-reference comparisons, all smooth
shifts, burst saturation, tuning endpoints, and 10000 off-state identities.
Additional ASan+UBSan actual-C binaries cover both config defaults crossed
with absent/sched_bore=0/sched_bore=1 command lines (six fresh processes),
invalid parser input preserving the default, the complete extracted shared
__sched_fork initializer with dirty idle/child state, and normal fork inheritance
on/off. Static checks verify init_idle uses the shared initializer without
inheritance and normal fork resets before inheriting. Existing off assertions
and algorithm tests are retained.
Non-PIE sanitizer binaries avoid host ASan shadow-map placement conflicts.
Static checks inspect sleep ordering/gating, real-runtime statements,
DEBUG-independent sysctl constants and immutable enable wiring.

POC optional boot default and tests
-----------------------------------
CONFIG_SCHED_POC_DEFAULT_ACTIVE depends on CONFIG_SCHED_POC_SELECTOR and
also defaults to n. poc_mode and both static keys always start at zero/off;
there is no static mode=2 initialization. In late_initcall poc_sysctl_init,
register_sysctl must succeed first. With the default-active option selected,
lock order is poc_control_lock mutex -> get_online_cpus -> existing
poc_set_mode(2) -> put_online_cpus -> mutex unlock (same as sysctl writes).
The setter disables readers, waits for synchronize_sched, publishes mode 0,
disables writers, waits again and clears the bitmap. It then enables writers,
resynchronizes online CPUs via on_each_cpu, publishes mode 2 and enables
readers. Failed registration returns -ENOMEM without locks or activation.
The runtime sched_poc_mode interface remains available for modes 0/1/2.

  python3 -I tools/testing/selftests/poc/run.py

Both default-off/default-active variants compile the actual registration and
sysctl C with UBSan. Tests require initially-off state, failed registration
staying off with no setter effects, successful locked activation through the
real setter/resync, idempotence, and sysctl parsing/transitions. Mocks assert
mutex-before-hotplug and writer-before-resync-before-reader ordering. Existing
off/shadow/active, rejection, fair-loop and full 0943 token-equivalence
assertions remain. The latter now strips the independent later BORE/Cambyses
config-guarded additions as well as POC to compare the all-disabled path.

These are host extraction tests, NOT a kernel configuration build, boot,
lockdep/KCSAN test, cgroup integration test or device latency/power benchmark.
Follow-up kernel builds (BORE n/y, DEBUG n/y, SCHEDSTATS n/y, group scheduling)
and on-device testing remain necessary before deployment. No kernel build
is performed by this test target. The test is standalone; the top-level
selftests Makefile is intentionally untouched to respect file ownership.
